# readings.ever — a summary over a list of sensor readings.
#
#   python3 2-interpreter-python/ever_cli.py run examples/readings.ever
#
# The same program as readings.ezr, in the grammar `ever run` actually
# speaks. The two lineages still split on binding — `let x = v in body`
# is an expression in the forge's core, `let x = v` is a statement here,
# and `ever run` has no `in` at all — so this file still will not run
# on `ezrun.py`, whose evaluator now speaks the CORE side of that split
# but was never taught v4.10's statement grammar. readings.ezr, in the
# other direction, now runs on both. See CORE.md.
#
# Two things worth watching.
#
# There is no `len`, `head` or `tail` here: v4.10's builtins are dot,
# exp, mse and sigmoid, so walking a list means indexing it. That is a
# real difference, not a shorthand.
#
# And the recursion counts DOWN, which is load bearing rather than
# stylistic. Anchoring needs a parameter that strictly decreases in
# every self-call; an index counting up has none, so it stays capped at
# floor(pi) = 3 and a six-element list comes back z. Measured:
#
#   def up(a, i, n) = if i >= n then 0 else a[i] + up(a, i + 1, n)
#   up(xs, 0, 6)   ->  z [0/256]        i increases; nothing decreases
#   down(xs, 5)    ->  63 [256/256]     i decreases; anchored, runs
#
# The language will tell you this itself if you ask it through ezrun:
# the refusal names the parameter that went the wrong way.

let readings = [12, 7, 19, 3, 14, 8]

def total(xs, i)    = if i < 0 then 0 else xs[i] + total(xs, i - 1)

def biggest(xs, i)  = if i <= 0 then xs[0]
                      else (if xs[i] > biggest(xs, i - 1)
                            then xs[i] else biggest(xs, i - 1))

def smallest(xs, i) = if i <= 0 then xs[0]
                      else (if xs[i] < smallest(xs, i - 1)
                            then xs[i] else smallest(xs, i - 1))

ever count   = 6
ever sum     = total(readings, 5)
ever mean    = sum / count
ever hi      = biggest(readings, 5)
ever lo      = smallest(readings, 5)
ever span    = hi - lo

# Reading past the end is not an error to be caught, it is a value: z,
# at 0/256. It can be carried, stored and compared like any other — and
# anything computed from it stays z, which is the point.
ever missing = readings[9]

show count
show sum
show mean
show hi
show lo
show span
show missing
